1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:06,780
Here's some fun math to ruin your day: The chance your vote decides an election is one in sixty million.

2
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:11,900
The value if your candidate wins is ten thousand dollars (in policies you prefer).

3
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:17,340
The expected value of voting is zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,580
The cost of voting (time, gas, finding parking) is fifty dollars.

5
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:27,740
The net value is negative forty-nine point nine nine nine eight three dollars.

6
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:35,370
Voting is literally irrational. You have better odds of dying in a car crash on the way to vote than your vote mattering.

7
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:40,760
Yet people get really mad when you point this out. Democracy.

8
00:00:41,160 --> 00:01:29,110
The Founding Fathers were brilliant men. They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

9
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:36,520
They did not anticipate Florida. Sixty-four percent of voters can't name the three branches of government.

10
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:40,520
These same voters choose who runs those branches. Not you, of course.

11
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:46,760
You're reading a book about international treaties. But statistically, the person sitting next to you thinks the judicial

12
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:55,540
branch is a type of tree. The Rational Ignorance Problem. The cost of being informed is one hundred plus hours researching

13
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:03,840
candidates and issues. The benefit of being informed is your one vote (worth zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars).

14
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:07,820
The rational decision is to stay ignorant and watch Netflix.

15
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:12,800
This isn't stupidity. It's the smartest decision your democracy makes possible.

16
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:18,780
Why spend one hundred hours researching healthcare policy when your vote won't change anything?

17
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:25,180
You could spend those hours learning Italian, and at least then you'd be able to order gelato while your democracy collapses.

18
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:34,260
What Democracy Should Look Like vs Reality. Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred seventy-nine policy

19
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:40,660
decisions over twenty years. The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually happens?

20
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,580
Zero. Percent. When economic elites want something? Seventy-eight percent of the time.

21
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:53,200
You have the same political influence as your houseplant. Slightly less, actually.

22
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:57,080
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

23
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:05,220
Your institutions tell the whole story in their names. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

24
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:09,540
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

25
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,090
And a "Department of Justice" that (you can see where this is going).

26
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:21,240
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same thirty percent chance of passing.

27
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:28,900
Democracy is a slot machine where every pull gives the same result but you keep playing because the machine has an American

28
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:36,420
flag on it. The Mathematics of Political Failure. Concentrated Benefits vs.

29
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:42,760
Diffuse Costs. Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

30
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,090
That's ten dollars per person. Will you spend weeks fighting to save ten dollars?

31
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:54,120
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

32
00:03:54,760 --> 00:04:04,080
Yes. Guess who wins? This pattern repeats everywhere: Military contractors receive a nine hundred and ninety-nine billion

33
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:10,820
dollar concentrated benefit (thirty-seven percent of two point seven two trillion dollars in global spending).

34
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:18,800
Taxpayers pay three thousand dollars each in diffuse costs (nine hundred and ninety-nine billion dollars divided by three

35
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:24,120
hundred and thirty-three million Americans). The winner? Guess.

36
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:24,380
Pharma companies see five hundred billion dollars in concentrated profits.

37
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:24,380
Patients pay one thousand five hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

38
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:29,340
The winner is not you. Banks in two thousand eight received a seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated).

39
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:33,980
You paid two thousand one hundred dollars in taxes (diffuse).

40
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:45,200
The winner is the ones with yachts. How Money Buys Power. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

41
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,520
Only one in four studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

42
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:55,480
Bribery is what poor countries do. Rich countries have "campaign finance.".

43
00:04:56,760 --> 00:05:01,620
But they do something far more effective: they buy which politicians exist in the first place.

44
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:07,140
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand four were won by the highest spender.

45
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:12,920
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars.

46
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:18,780
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million dollars.

47
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:24,580
Why bribe a politician when you can just manufacture one who already agrees with you?

48
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:27,680
It's like complaining about the menu when you own the restaurant.

49
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:36,080
For the ones already in office, contributions buy something more subtle than votes: Access means your call gets returned,

50
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:42,340
and theirs goes to voicemail. Agenda control means your issue gets a hearing, while theirs gets buried.

51
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:46,440
Legislative language is the exact wording that creates loopholes.

52
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:54,160
Committee positions can be bought for four hundred fifty thousand dollars to party committees, which buys a seat where bills

53
00:05:54,160 --> 00:06:00,960
live or die. You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

54
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:09,260
The Lobbying return on investment, or R.O.I.: one thousand eight hundred ten to one Returns.

55
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:14,700
The most profitable investment in America isn't stocks, real estate, or crypto.

56
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:20,740
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

57
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:24,880
They get one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

58
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:34,420
The R.O.I. is one thousand eight hundred ten to one. Meanwhile: Breast cancer has great lobbyists which results in massive

59
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:40,000
funding. Heart disease kills more people, but worse lobbyists means less funding.

60
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:45,940
Rare pediatric diseases have no lobbyists, which means no funding.

61
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:51,680
Democracy doesn't allocate resources by who's dying. It allocates by who can afford the best lobbyist.

62
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:56,240
If your disease can't hire a P.R. firm, it's not a real disease.

63
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:01,900
It's just dying. Why Nothing Ever Changes: A Mathematical Proof.

64
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:07,880
The number of Americans who want lower drug prices is three hundred thirty million.

65
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:16,500
The money they contribute to this cause is zero dollars. The number of pharma executives who want high drug prices is one

66
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,960
thousand. The money they contribute is four point eight eight billion dollars.

67
00:07:19,390 --> 00:07:25,040
One thousand is greater than three hundred thirty million (when you multiply by money).

68
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:31,700
That's not corruption. That's math. Why Politicians Don't Care About You (With Math!).

69
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:37,940
A politician needs three things: Number one: votes (from idiots).

70
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:44,300
Number two: money (from corporations). Number three: media coverage (from other corporations).

71
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:48,920
You provide one one hundred fifty millionth of item number one.

72
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:53,980
Corporations provide one hundred percent of items number two and number three.

73
00:07:54,540 --> 00:07:58,500
Who do you think they listen to? The Iron Law of Oligarchy.

74
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:03,360
Every democratic organization eventually becomes an oligarchy.

75
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:09,500
Political parties, unions, non-profits, your homeowners association, the P.T.A.

76
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:14,760
Sharon from the bake sale committee is running it exactly like a Medici, and she knows it.

77
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:21,520
Democracy doesn't prevent oligarchy. It gives oligarchy a suit and a certificate that says "elected.".

78
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:25,980
Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

79
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:31,620
Average time congressmen spend on: Fundraising: four hours a day.

80
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:36,080
Reading bills: zero hours a day. Actual governing: Whatever's left.

81
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:44,700
They don't read what they vote on. The Patriot Act: three hundred forty-two pages, voted on forty-five minutes after release.

82
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:50,730
That's seven point six pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers can't do that.

83
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:57,040
The Affordable Care Act: two thousand seven hundred pages, "we have to pass it to see what's in it".

84
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:03,680
These people control nuclear weapons. Sleep tight. Congressional Committees: The Menu.

85
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:12,300
Committees control which bills live or die. Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much for the

86
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:19,500
party, or lose your assignment. Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal

87
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:24,360
"wall of shame". It's like a restaurant where the waiters have to pay for their own sections.

88
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:31,160
Here's the menu: The House Speaker seat costs the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, or D.C.C.C., thirty-one million

89
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:36,320
dollars and the National Republican Congressional Committee, or N.R.C.C., twenty million dollars, because it controls the

90
